WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.233
MAX SALTONSTALL: Hi,
I'm Max Saltonstall,

00:00:02.233 --> 00:00:04.650
and today I'm going to talk
through some simple things you

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:07.080
can do to secure your
workloads and sensitive data

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:09.377
in the cloud, while
still keeping it private.

00:00:09.377 --> 00:00:10.960
We're going to talk
about how shielded

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:14.440
VMs and confidential VMs
can help you do this.

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:17.070
Let's start with creating a
shielded VM in the Google Cloud

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:18.030
console.

00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:20.700
Shielded VMs are virtual
machine instances

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:23.850
that are hardened with a set
of easily configurable security

00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:26.700
features to ensure that
when your VM boots,

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:29.730
it's running a verified
bootloader and kernel.

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:31.860
Using shielded VMs
helps protect workloads

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:34.680
from threats, like remote
attacks, privilege escalation,

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:36.510
and malicious insiders.

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:38.460
And yes, everything
we do here could also

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:41.070
be done through G Cloud
and the command line.

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:43.830
Starting at Compute
Engine, to to VM instances

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:45.500
and then Create.

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:50.700
Give it a name, then go down
to Boot Disk and click Change.

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:51.570
Choose any image.

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:55.470
They're all shielded by default.
Close that window with Select

00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:57.840
at the bottom, and then go
down to the tabs labeled

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:01.710
Management security disks, et
cetera, and click Security.

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:04.590
You can see the default
shielded VM settings.

00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:07.560
Let's enable Secure Boot
here for extra protection,

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:10.090
and then click
Create to spin it up.

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:12.420
Turning on Secure Boot
gives us added protection

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:15.390
from boot and kernel
level malware.

00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:17.520
Once it's ready,
click on the new VM

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:22.380
and click on Stackdriver Logging
under Logs on the Detail page.

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:25.890
At the Logs page, you can filter
for just VM integrity logs

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:28.080
by clicking on the
All Logs dropdown,

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:32.280
and choosing shielded underscore
VM underscore integrity.

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:34.170
Now you can see
just the shielded VM

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:35.920
offense for this instance.

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:38.070
Let's dig in further.

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:42.090
Click into the log for
Early Boot Report Event.

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:45.000
Then expand the JSON payload,
then the Early Boot Report

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:46.920
Event inside that.

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:49.020
You can confirm the
VM's integrity state

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:51.600
from the early boot perspective
from the line showing

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:54.600
policy evaluation past is true.

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:57.120
Early Boot encompasses
firmware and other drivers

00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:59.550
loaded during the boot
process of the VM.

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:01.530
Back to the logs list,
open up the log that

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:04.420
starts with boot counter,
then expand JSON payload,

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:06.150
then Late Boot
Report Event shows

00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:08.669
you details about the integrity
of the rest of your kernel

00:02:08.669 --> 00:02:09.900
drivers.

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:12.960
Once again, you can confirm
the boot integrity of the VM

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:16.830
from the line Policy
Evaluation Past being true.

00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:19.170
That shows that the VM
passed all late boot checks,

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:22.650
and is running what we expect it
to be running, no shenanigans.

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:25.920
Now head into Cloud Shell
to disable Secure Boot so we

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:27.770
can test this integrity check.

00:02:27.770 --> 00:02:28.270
Uh-oh.

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:33.990
First, stop the VM with G Cloud.

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:39.800
Next, disable Secure Boot
using G Cloud Update.

00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:46.490
Now restart the VM, and
you can see in the console

00:02:46.490 --> 00:02:49.280
that the integrity verification
has failed by examining

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:53.090
the same logs as before.

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:55.677
Finally, make a new integrity
baseline with G Cloud

00:02:55.677 --> 00:02:57.260
so you can compare
future measurements

00:02:57.260 --> 00:02:59.033
against the current state.

00:02:59.033 --> 00:03:00.950
Pretend that you already
triaged this failure,

00:03:00.950 --> 00:03:03.097
and consider it acceptable.

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:07.280
With this new
baseline established,

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:09.320
go check the integrity
state of the shielded VM

00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:11.510
again in Stackdriver Logging.

00:03:11.510 --> 00:03:15.440
Make sure you click Load Newer
Logs to get the latest results.

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:17.970
Looking at the late
boot report event again,

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:20.660
we can see that the policy
evaluation passed is true

00:03:20.660 --> 00:03:23.090
once again, showing that
we have met our integrity

00:03:23.090 --> 00:03:27.920
standards, the ones we
just lowered, of course.

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:30.230
Finally, time to look at
these metrics over time

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:31.790
via monitoring.

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:33.950
Open up monitoring, and
head to Metrics Explorer.

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:39.060
Under Find resource,
type, and metric,

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:43.650
choose the resource GCE VM
Instance and the metric Early

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:44.862
Boot validation.

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:51.191
Then add a second metric,
Late Boot validation.

00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:56.910
We've got our graph, though
it's going to make more

00:03:56.910 --> 00:04:00.150
sense as a stacked bar graph.

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:00.973
Looks great.

00:04:00.973 --> 00:04:02.640
Now you can keep track
of your integrity

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:05.153
over time with gorgeous colors.

00:04:05.153 --> 00:04:07.320
When our customers are ready
to move their workloads

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:09.330
to the cloud, one of
the biggest challenges

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:11.940
is securely processing
sensitive data in the cloud

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:13.380
while keeping it private.

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:16.473
Google Cloud offers solutions
to securely bring sensitive data

00:04:16.473 --> 00:04:18.390
to the cloud, using
encryption to protect data

00:04:18.390 --> 00:04:21.690
in transit for offline
or online ingestion.

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:23.730
But when we have to
actually use the data,

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:26.490
it's much tougher to
keep it encrypted.

00:04:26.490 --> 00:04:28.500
With confidential
computing now in beta,

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:30.210
Google Cloud lets you
take data security

00:04:30.210 --> 00:04:31.980
a step further by
encrypting data

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:35.370
while it's being processed
in a Google data center.

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:36.990
Confidential
computing environments

00:04:36.990 --> 00:04:39.150
can now enable
encrypted data to be

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:41.100
processed in memory
without exposing it

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:42.558
to the rest of the system.

00:04:42.558 --> 00:04:44.100
And this can be done
without the need

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:47.420
to make any changes in
your workload or code.

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:49.170
Let's take a look at
the product by making

00:04:49.170 --> 00:04:52.050
a new confidential computing
VM which contains a shielded

00:04:52.050 --> 00:04:54.570
VM to perform the computation.

00:04:54.570 --> 00:04:58.050
Inside Compute Engine,
create a new VM instance.

00:04:58.050 --> 00:05:00.935
Enable Confidential Computing
service with a checkbox.

00:05:00.935 --> 00:05:03.560
Note what changes are being made
to your VMs with this setting,

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:05.460
then click Enable.

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:06.990
You can run any
workload you want.

00:05:06.990 --> 00:05:08.640
Your VM is now
much safer and more

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:10.560
secure with all of
the memory encrypted,

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:12.555
so your data is safe in use.

00:05:12.555 --> 00:05:14.430
But what about instance
templates and groups,

00:05:14.430 --> 00:05:15.510
you might ask?

00:05:15.510 --> 00:05:17.227
Well, we've got you covered.

00:05:17.227 --> 00:05:18.810
The same confidential
computing option

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:20.550
is available for
instance templates,

00:05:20.550 --> 00:05:22.620
and you can either use
one of our premade images

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:25.140
or your own custom image
that best fits your workload.

00:05:35.990 --> 00:05:38.840
Now my template defines a
shielded confidential VM setup,

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:41.300
and I can go to instance groups
to make a managed instance

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:42.342
group with that template.

00:05:47.660 --> 00:05:50.720
Beep, bop, boop, we're done,
with a set of instances auto

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:54.080
scaling for my needs,
secure and ready to work.

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:55.790
Confidential
computing environments

00:05:55.790 --> 00:05:57.470
can now enable
encrypted data to be

00:05:57.470 --> 00:05:59.450
processed in memory
without exposing it

00:05:59.450 --> 00:06:00.670
to the rest of the system.

00:06:00.670 --> 00:06:02.212
And this can be done
without the need

00:06:02.212 --> 00:06:05.160
to make any changes
in your applications.

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:06.470
Thanks for joining me today.

00:06:06.470 --> 00:06:08.655
Enjoy shielded VMs and
confidential computing

00:06:08.655 --> 00:06:11.030
for your workloads on cloud,
and check out the link below

00:06:11.030 --> 00:06:12.230
to get started.

00:06:12.230 --> 00:06:15.280
